2 NOTE Ibiraçu was founded in 1877 by a group of 54 families who departed from the Genoa region of northe
3 CONC rn Italy. After a voyage by steam boat of 35 days the immigrants arrived in the state capital of Vit
3 CONC ória, were briefly quarantined, then traveled by boat and canoe to colonize the headwaters of the Pi
3 CONC raqueaçu River. The area they settled was then known as Santa Cruz Colonial Nucleus (Núcleo Coloni
3 CONC al Santa Cruz), since the most accessible town to it was Santa Cruz, at the mouth of the Piraqueaç
3 CONC u. The early settlers of the municipality were affected by the yellow fever outbreak of 1895, whi
3 CONC ch both suspended further settlement of Espírito Santo and killed 41 members.
3 CONT The capital of this area was first known as Conde d'Eu, and was later known by many names such as Bo
3 CONC caiúva (after the proclamation of the republic), Vila Guaraná (after it became a municipality), P
3 CONC au Gigante in 1892 (meaning giant tree, due to a large tree in the region), and only in 1942 did bo
3 CONC th the town and municipality gain Ibiraçu as their name. In recent history it is notable to menti
3 CONC on the separation of João Neiva municipality.
3 CONT Ibiraçu was called Pau Gigante until 1949.
3 CONT
3 CONT The cultivation of coffee and eucalyptus forms the basis of the economy of Ibiraçu.
