1 TITL History of João Neiva, Espírito Santo, Brazil
1 AUTH Getúlio Vargas Foundation
1 TEXT https://www.joaoneiva.es.gov.br/v1/?page=conteudo&subfrom=MUNIC%C3%8DPIO&pagina=05e735a3b2
2 CONT
2 CONT With the arrival of the first families arise the villages of Acioli (1887) and Demétrio Ribeiro (189
2 CONC 1).
2 CONT At the beginning of the century, the engineer and federal deputy from Bahia, João Augusto Neiva, fou
2 CONC ght hard in the Federal Chamber for the installation of the Diamantina Railroad, belonging to Compan
2 CONC hia Estrada de Ferro Vitória x Minas.
2 CONT With the installation of the Railroad, the Railway Station was inaugurated, inaugurated on Decemb
2 CONC er 20, 1905. The land for the work was donated by Mr. Negri Orestes.
2 CONT Pedro Nolasco, who was the founder of the construction of the Railroad, to honor the federal depu
2 CONC ty João Augusto Neiva, gave the Station the name of João Neiva. It is around the Station that the vi
2 CONC llage "João Neiva" appears.
2 CONT On December 30, 1921, João Neiva, through Law no. 1305 is elevated to District. After several attemp
2 CONC ts at emancipation, finally on March 30, 1988, the Legislative Assembly met again, to confirm the re
2 CONC sult of the plebiscite, confirming the same. On that day, the Deputy Governor of the State Carlos Al
2 CONC berto Batista da Cunha, temporarily acting as Governor of the State, signed Law No. 4076 publish
2 CONC ed in the Official Gazette of May 11, 1988, creating the Municipality of João Neiva.
2 CONT On November 15, 1988, the first election was held in the Municipality. Having been elected Mayor, Al
2 CONC uyzio Morelatto, for Vice Mayor, José Anízio Ivo Secomandi, and for the first City Council the Alder
2 CONC men: Alécio Jocimar Fávaro, Antônio Pandolfi, Augusto Tessarolo, Ayrton Fornaciari, Edson Luiz Dal P
2 CONC iaz Coutinho, José Carlos Alves José Domingos Del Pupo, José Francisco Grippa, José Geraldo Colomb
2 CONC o, Jurandir Mattos do Nascimento, Maria Luiza Casoti Louzada, Natalino Antonio Gasparine and Waldem
2 CONC ar de Barros.
2 CONT With the inauguration of those elected on November 15, 1988, the Municipality was officially install
2 CONC ed on January 1, 1989, at a ceremony held at the João Neiva Community Center, presided over by Dr. M
2 CONC achado de Souza, Judge of the Comarca de Ibiraçu .
2 CONT
2 CONT COLONIZATION
2 CONT The colonization of the region where the municipality of João Neiva is located began with the arriv
2 CONC al of immigrants, mainly Italians. The first families came in the year 1877. But the great levies to
2 CONC ok a little longer to arrive.
2 CONT On February 24, 1874 the ship "Sofia" anchored in the Port of Vitória. The Tyroleses were broug
2 CONC ht in by Mr. Pietro Tabachi, who had signed a contract.
2 CONT The immigrants who were destined to the colonial nuclei of Santa Cruz (Conde d'Eu), Acioli de Vascon
2 CONC celos and Demétrio Ribeiro, which today constitute the present municipalities of Ibiraçu and João Ne
2 CONC iva, disembarked in Vitória, followed later in smaller vapors to Santa Cruz . From there they climb
2 CONC ed in canoes, the Piraqueaçu River to the Port of Santana, in Córrego Fundo, from where they continu
2 CONC ed, on foot, to the various sessions of these nuclei.
2 CONT The foundation of the colonial nuclei Acioli de Vasconcelos and Antônio Prado in 1887, Demétrio Ribe
2 CONC iro in 1891 and Moniz Freire in 1894 had the objective of resolving the ancient and important probl
2 CONC em of the colonization of the Rio Doce, already tried so often and always with failure.
2 CONT Dr. Antônio Francisco de Atayde was the founder of the Colonial Nucleus Acioli de Vasconcelos, and t
2 CONC he name given to the Nucleus is a tribute to the then Inspector General of Land and Colonization, li
2 CONC eutenant colonel Francisco de Barros and Acioli de Vasconcelos, veteran of the War of the Paraguay.
2 CONT Officially, however, the first establishments of settlers in the colonial nucleus, later named Acio
2 CONC li de Vasconcelos, were effected by the engineer himself Francisco de Atayde, from August of 1888.
2 CONT However, the largest contingents of Italian immigrants established in the Colonial Acioli de Vasconc
2 CONC elos Nucleus took place during the months of January, September and October of 1889 and beginni
2 CONC ng of 1892.
2 CONT Most of these immigrants arrived in Vitória aboard the steamer "Estrela", on March 29, 1889, from R
2 CONC io de Janeiro, and some in the "Adria", directly from Genoa - Italy, on December 27, 1888 .
2 CONT Bibliographical data of Lieutenant-Colonel Acioli de Vasconcelos: the name of the Colonial Nucleus A
2 CONC cioli de Vasconcelos was given in honor of Lieutenant-Colonel Francisco de Barros and Acioli de Vasc
2 CONC oncelos, who held the position of General Inspector of Land and Colonization at the time of the foun
2 CONC dation of this nucleus.
2 CONT Acioli de Vasconcelos, died on September 26, 1907, before turning 61 in Rio de Janeiro, and the "Diá
2 CONC rio da Manhã", of this Capital, on October 9 of that year, published a note communicating his dea
2 CONC th and presenting some information about your life:
2 CONT  - volunteer of the War of Paraguay;
2 CONT  - participant in the battles of Estero-Belaco, Passo da Pátria, May 24, Avaí and Itororó;
2 CONT  - appointed Secretary of the Navy Arsenal;
2 CONT  - appointed as Head of Section of the Secretariat of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Commer
2 CONC ce and Public Works for his private friend, Visconde do Rio Branco;
2 CONT  - appointed by the Minister Afonso Pena to assume the General Inspection of Lands and Colonizatio
2 CONC n, where he had the opportunity to contribute to the good management of the immigration and coloniza
2 CONC tion service in our country, and particularly in our State.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE FIRST VICTIMS
2 CONT As soon as they had been established in the Acioli Nucleus of Vasconcelos, the immigrants who arriv
2 CONC ed there in 1889, suffered serious illnesses, and some died soon in the first contacts with the n
2 CONC ew land.
2 CONT These victims were, in large measure, unlucky enough to be established in the most unhealthy regi
2 CONC on of the nucleus, which comprised a vast expanse of land, between the mouth of Esperança Strea
2 CONC m, on the Pau Gigante, to the vicinity of the Doce River, including Lagoa of Coffee.
2 CONT It was a low, marshy, disease-infested region.
2 CONT The colonization in this part of the nucleus was processed with sacrifice of many human lives and d
2 CONC id not obtain the expected result.
2 CONT
2 CONT OLD TEACHERS
2 CONT  - Sebastiana Pereira Grillo, Djanira Ferraz Coutinho, Dina Pinheiro, Maria Bittencourt Rocha (Do
2 CONC na Cotinha), Francisca Eulália Santana (Dona Chiquinha), Fernandina das Neves Raizer and the siste
2 CONC rs Adelaide and Olga Raizer, the latter two being the only ones born in Acioli.
2 CONT  - Sebastiana Pereira Grillo, graduated in December 1916, and on February 7, 1917 was appointed to a
2 CONC dminister the School of the female of Acioli, and entered into exercise on 22 of the same month.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE FIRST PHYSICIAN
2 CONT One of the first doctors of the place was Dr. Francisco de Paula Amarante Filho, a native of Rio Gra
2 CONC nde do Sul.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE FIRST TRADERS
2 CONT One of the first to settle in Acioli was Coriolano Pereira, who arrived there in the first deca
2 CONC de of this century, coming from Nova Almeida, and ended up becoming one of the most important peop
2 CONC le of the place.
2 CONT Next, the following merchants were established: Manoel Alvarenga Pimentel, Narciso Machado, Francis
2 CONC co Campostrini and many others.
2 CONT
2 CONT TRIUMPH BAR
2 CONT The Colonial Acioli de Vasconcelos Nucleus was slowly establishing itself in its early years of foun
2 CONC dation, mainly due to the region's unhealthiness, the precariousness of the roads and the lack of mo
2 CONC re assistance.
2 CONT The first section of this nucleus to present development was Barra do Triunfo, where the farm found
2 CONC ed by Giuseppe Battisti existed.
2 CONT It acquired land in the Rio Ubás and Barra do Triunfo, devoting itself to agriculture (cultivating c
2 CONC offee, sugar cane and cereals, also assembling mills for the processing of these products), produci
2 CONC ng high quality beer, with monthly production of 3,000 thousand bottles, and to trade; he also act
2 CONC ed as a carpenter. It was on this farm also that the inhabitants of the nucleus met on festive occas
2 CONC ions.
2 CONT With the transfer of Giuseppe Battisti to Pau Gigante, where he came to occupy important position
2 CONC s, including that of Mayor of the Municipality, the direction of business in Barra do Triunfo was un
2 CONC der the responsibility of his brother Guglielmo Battisti, whose name was anointed for Guilherme Bapt
2 CONC ista , to avoid political persecution, of Getúlio Vargas' policy.
2 CONT Guilherme Baptista achieved extraordinary development in the region.
2 CONT He founded in 1907 the "Lira Triunfense" - music band, having as first master Mr. Antônio Barcelos.
2 CONT In 1915, he founded the "Peasant" - soccer team, also participating as a player.
2 CONT In the 1920s, Guilherme Baptista bought a truck and a car, the first to drive in the region, a
2 CONC nd at the same time built the road that connects Barra do Triunfo to Acioli.
2 CONT Still in this decade, in partnership with his nephew Florencio, son of Giovanni Baptista, he bui
2 CONC lt a dam on the Ubás River and inaugurated the Hydroelectric Plant that for several years, until t
2 CONC he advent of Excelsa, provided light to Acioli and surroundings. It was also acquired the first rad
2 CONC io of the place.
2 CONT This successful Italian immigrant died on September 27, 1941, and was buried in Bar of Triumph, leav
2 CONC ing a widow, Maria Casotti and the following children: Hilario, Hortêncio, Theodolinda, Maurílio, Eu
2 CONC clides, Aldemira, Hermes, Eulália, Davina, Iracema and Euflodísio.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE CHURCH OF OUR LADY OF CARAVAGGIO
2 CONT The Church of Our Lady of Caravaggio, on the property of the family Ceccato, between Acioli and Bar
2 CONC ra do Triunfo, was built by Ceccato Giovanni Pietro and annually, on May 26, the day dedicated to th
2 CONC is venerated saint, there is performed the beautiful and lively party, in his honor.
2 CONT The Caravaggio sanctuary in Italy, where the pious young woman lived, is in the Diocese of Cremon
2 CONC a, 80 km from Milan.
2 CONT
2 CONT COLONIAL NÚCLEO DEMÉTRIO RIBEIRO
2 CONT Founded in 1891, it consists of the following sections: Crubixá, Thirteen of June, Clotário, São Car
2 CONC los, Alto Bergamo, São Benedito, Tranquilo and Treze de Maio.
2 CONT The foundation of this nucleus was influenced by the immigrant Negri Orestes.
2 CONT As for the name of the Nucleus, it was given in honor of the Minister of Agriculture, Commerce and P
2 CONC ublic Works, Demétrio Ribeiro, of the Provisional Government of the Republic, headed by Marshal Teod
2 CONC oro da Fonseca.
2 CONT The nucleus had at that time a zinc-covered shed for immigrants' lodging and a house that serv
2 CONC ed as an office and pharmacy, with accommodations for the doctor and that, besides these Governme
2 CONC nt buildings, there were still four business houses, two bakeries, two blacksmiths, two water power
2 CONC ed mills for coffee pillar and cane mill.
2 CONT Its main crops were coffee, sugar cane, corn, rice and potatoes.
2 CONT With the inauguration of the Vitória a Minas Railway Station in December 1905, which was only 6km fr
2 CONC om the village of Demétrio Ribeiro, the situation of this nucleus has improved considerably, d
2 CONC ue to the advantages offered by this new means of transportation.
2 CONT
2 CONT INAUGURATION OF WATER AND LIGHT SERVICES
2 CONT In August 1924, under the initiative and resources of Mr. Giuseppe Battisti, the Water Service was i
2 CONC naugurated in the village of Pau Gigante, whose improvement, as it could not fail to be, was receiv
2 CONC ed with great enthusiasm and satisfaction by the local population.
2 CONT Less than two years later, on May 23, 1926, the Hydroelectric Plant was inaugurated in the same vill
2 CONC age.
2 CONT This plant installed in Cachoeira do Barro was practically destroyed in the night of 17 to 18 Novemb
2 CONC er 1942, by a water spout, which was constituted in the greatest disaster recorded in all the histo
2 CONC ry of this Municipality.
2 CONT At that time, almost all the bridges and plantations were destroyed and many houses disappeared, dra
2 CONC gged by the flood, causing the homelessness of many people and some deaths.
2 CONT The trains, the only means of transport of the place, stopped to circulate once some rails were upro
2 CONC oted.
2 CONT Mr. Ayrton Bonesi, Mayor of the Municipality, from 1942 to 1947, fought all his administration to re
2 CONC build the Municipality. However the problems were many and the resources available were insignifican
2 CONC t.
2 CONT It reopened the Santa Teresinha Hydroelectric Plant on October 6, 1946, shortly before leaving the C
2 CONC ity Hall.
2 CONT Years later the Municipality happened to be served by Escelsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT INAUGURATION OF THE HYDROELECTRIC POWER PLANT OF JOÃO NEIVA
2 CONT At the initiative of the firm of the brothers NEGRI, the Hydroelectric Plant of João Neiva was inaug
2 CONC urated, on December 13, 1924.
2 CONT
2 CONT SCHOOL - SENAI
2 CONT In order to attend to the children of the railroad workers of the workshops of João Neiva, the Lic
2 CONC eu Pedro Nolasco was founded, directed and financed by an Association of Railways.
2 CONT In 1947 the works of the "SENAI RAILWAYS" School of Porto Velho and João Neiva were started, for t
2 CONC he formation of craftsman of the workshops of the Road.
2 CONT In 1949 the first school period began at the SENAI RAILWAY SCHOOL of João Neiva, which counted on t
2 CONC he professional courses of Mechanical Turning, Caldeiraria and Ferraria. The duration of the cour
2 CONC se was three years. In the latter, the students took internships at the Locomotives workshops, whe
2 CONC re they would work after graduating.
2 CONT On August 28, 1948, Cia Vale do Rio Doce founded the "TALMO LUIZ SILVA" TRAINING CENTER, with the pu
2 CONC rpose of training professionals to attend to the company itself, maintaining, through agreement, SEN
2 CONC AI teaching standards.
2 CONT In 1991, with the deactivation of CVRD's workshops in João Neiva, all the assets were donated to t
2 CONC he João Neiva City Hall, creating the EDUCATIONAL FOUNDATION "Dr. HILDO GARCIA ", whose main objecti
2 CONC ve was to maintain the" Talmo Luiz Silva "Training Center; the foundation was maintained by the Muni
2 CONC cipal Government of João Neiva, maintaining the same standards.
2 CONT There was a disruption of the agreement with SENAI and the signing of the protocol of intentions wi
2 CONC th CEFET-ES (Federal Center for Technological Education of Espírito Santo) in 1999.
2 CONT Currently, CEFORP is working to bring new courses and principally to implement technical vocation
2 CONC al courses in the Municipality, providing the community with greater comfort and reliability in prof
2 CONC essional education in our region.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE DEACTIVATION OF THE OFFICES OF JOÃO NEIVA
2 CONT By not meeting the technical conditions demanded by the company, the workshops suffered several thre
2 CONC ats of deactivation through the ages. More than once the works of expansion and renovation were carr
2 CONC ied out, in order to adjust them to the new requirements. With the beginning of the export of o
2 CONC re in 1940, the increase in the number of trains and the weight of the cargo transported, the numb
2 CONC er of accidents increased, causing a workload to the workshops.
2 CONT A major reform was imposed and began in 1943. However, after a few years, reforms have become obsole
2 CONC te. Despite the appeals and cries of the whole community, it was decided that the workshops of Jo
2 CONC ão Neiva would be deactivated; and on April 1, 1991, employees of João Neiva's workshops began the
2 CONC ir activities in the workshops located in the Tubarão port area of ​​Companhia Vale do Rio Doc
2 CONC e, in Vitória.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE FINAL APITHER
2 CONT On July 25, 1991, the locomotive that came to João Neiva was totally weakened and rebuilt; the one t
2 CONC hat did so much for the benefit of Companhia Vale do Rio Doce.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY OF THE JOURNALIST AND DEPUTY JOÃO AUGUSTO NEIVA
2 CONT João Augusto Neiva was born in the town of Barra do Rio Grande de São Francisco, in the current muni
2 CONC cipality of Barra (BA), on March 21, 1847, son of João Augusto Neiva and Joana Cândida de Castr
2 CONC o. Of humble origin, he took the humanities course in Salvador and soon began to work. He started o
2 CONC ut as a newspaper typographer, but soon revealed a talent for reporter. In 1864 he became edit
2 CONC or of The Constitutional, an organ of popularization of the Conservative Party; when it was replac
2 CONC ed by the Jornal da Bahia, was kept in its functions. In September 1868, he was appointed to the Po
2 CONC st Office by the Ministry of Transport and Public Works, and remained in office until 1879. During t
2 CONC his period he was also administrator of the São João Theater.
2 CONT In 1879 he was appointed officer of the secretariat of the Provincial Assembly of Bahia; was later p
2 CONC romoted to general director, position in which he would retire in 1895. He initiated the political r
2 CONC ace electing himself provincial deputy by the 14th district for the legislature 1882-1883. With t
2 CONC he implantation of the Republic he became a Federal Republican Party and became director of the Gaze
2 CONC ta da Bahia. He was elected state deputy for the 1893-1894 legislature and was elected first secreta
2 CONC ry of the Legislative Assembly. After the breakup of the Republican Federalist Party, on April 15, 1
2 CONC 894, he joined the Federal Republican Party, in the wing headed by the adviser Luís Viana, hence cal
2 CONC led the "Vianist."
2 CONT Also in 1894 he was elected federal deputy, taking a seat in the Chamber of Deputies, in Rio de Jane
2 CONC iro, then Federal District. Several times re-elected, he would exercise successive mandates for 15 u
2 CONC ninterrupted years, until 1908. In the Chamber of Deputies, he was part of the Finance Committee, am
2 CONC ong others. His conduct as a deputy, always behaving with loyalty, sincerity and kindness in the tre
2 CONC atment, guaranteed the respect and admiration of the other members of the National Congress, in su
2 CONC ch a way that his influence surpassed the limits of the benches and overlapped that of the leade
2 CONC rs of each one of them. This provoked in several leaders, mainly in the one of the Bahia bench, fe
2 CONC ar and distrust, since in more than one opportunity its orientations were disconsidered, preferri
2 CONC ng the parliamentarians to attend the personal request of João Neiva.
2 CONT In spite of this prestige, in the election for the legislature 1909-1911 it failed to obtain the rec
2 CONC ognition of its mandate. Discontented with his compulsory departure, on October 6, 1909 deputies dec
2 CONC ided to appoint him head of the House Debates Section. On December 15, she was promoted to superinte
2 CONC ndent of the Mesa, position in which she retired. As a journalist he was also director of the newspa
2 CONC pers Public Interest, Lighthouse, The Courier, and Telegraph. He was a meritorious partner of the Ly
2 CONC ceum of Arts and Crafts of Bahia, and an honorary member of the Bahia Typographical Associatio
2 CONC n. He died in Rio de Janeiro on August 21, 1923. He married Ana Adelaide Neiva with whom he had fo
2 CONC ur children. Among them stood out Artur Neiva, who was noted as one of the greatest authorities in m
2 CONC alaria, being the first to publish work on the disease;
2 CONT
